          "answer_excerpt": "ALPHA code is MAPLE-902657.\nBETA code is RIVER-435643.\nGAMMA code is CLOUD-912485.\n\n# High-Performance LLM Inference: A Technical Deep Dive into Continuous Batching, KV Caches, and Advanced Decoding Strategies\n\nThe deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) in production environments presents a unique set of engineering challenges that differ significantly from traditional machine learning inference tasks. Unlike classification or regression models, which typically process fixed-size inputs and produce fixed-size outputs in a single forward pass, LLMs are autoregressive generative models. They generate text token-by-token, where each new token depends on the entire sequence of previously generated tokens. This sequential dependency, combined with the massive parameter counts of modern models (often ranging from 7 billion to hundreds of billions of parameters), creates a complex bottleneck in both memory bandwidth and computational efficiency. To serve these models at scale with low latency and high throughput, modern inference servers employ a suite of sophisticated optimization techniques. This tutorial explores the core mechanisms that enable high-performance LLM serving: the Key-Value (KV) Cache, Continuous Batching, Prefix Reuse, and Speculative Decoding, while analyzing the critical trade-offs between latency and throughput.\n\n## 1. The Fundamental Bottleneck: Memory Bandwidth and the KV Cache\n\nTo understand why LLM inference is difficult, one must first understand the architecture of the Transformer model, specifically the attention mechanism. In a standard Transformer, the attention layer computes a weighted sum of value vectors based on the similarity between query vectors and key vectors. For a sequence of length $N$, the self-attention mechanism requires computing an $N \\times N$ attention matrix. During the prefill phase (processing the input prompt), this is computationally intensive but parallelizable. However, during the generation phase (decoding), the model generates one token at a time.\n\nIf we were to recompute the attention for the entire sequence at every step, the computational cost would grow quadratically with the sequence length, making long-context generation prohibitively slow. To mitigate this, inference engines utilize the **Key-Value (KV) Cache**.\n\n### How the KV Cache Works\n\nThe KV Cache stores the key ($K$) and value ($V$) vectors computed for all previous tokens in the sequence. When the model generates the next token, it only needs to compute the query ($Q$), key ($K",
